### Get the pack for a UTC calendar date
#
# ONE OF TWO routed endpoints in this API (template.yaml, GetPackByDateFunction -> Path
# /packs/{date}, under BasePath /v1 on stage v1). The other is GET /dictionary/{version}, at the
# bottom of this file, which serves one static asset one puzzle type needs; everything a device needs
# for a DAY arrives here. There is no per-puzzle route and no puzzle is ever fetched on its own.
# Unauthenticated.
#
# BasePath is /v1 and the stage is v1, so the URL is https://lull-api.dbowland.com/v1/packs/{date} --
# and for the other route https://lull-api.dbowland.com/v1/dictionary/v1, where THE TWO v1s ARE
# UNRELATED: the first is the API's base path and the second is the dictionary's own content
# version, which moves on its own schedule.
#
# `{date}` is a UTC CALENDAR date, YYYY-MM-DD, and never a local-time date. A client east of UTC
# that derives it from a local midnight asks for the wrong day for part of every day.
#
# 200 -> { "complete": true, "date": "2026-08-20", "puzzles": [ ... ] }
#
# `complete` is the refetch signal, and it is RECOMPUTED on every RESPONSE rather than trusted from
# the stored item -- at every path a pack build can return from, all graded identically. The stored
# flag was frozen by whichever deploy's registry wrote it, and two ordinary events invalidate it
# without anything rewriting the pack:
#
#   * countPerDay SHRINKS on a deploy. Every already-stored pack of that type is now full, or
#     over-full, while its stored flag still says short. Nothing is missing, so nothing is
#     generated, so nothing is written -- a stored flag would never clear.
#   * a WRITE RACE is lost. What comes back is the pack the other run stored, carrying that run's
#     flag from that run's registry. It is regraded here before it goes out.
#
# Trusting the stored flag instead would serve a short day forever, or keep a client refetching a
# day that is already full.
#
# Recomputed against the registry AS IT APPLIED TO THAT DATE, which is not the same as the current
# registry. Each puzzle type declares the first pack date it applies to, and a type is ignored on
# every date before it. Without that, the day a new type shipped, every pack ever written would
# start reporting `false` at once -- and an incomplete pack is one this route will try to fill, so
# the entire archive would become eligible for generation on a single deploy.
#
# A type may also be declared best-effort, meaning it is allowed to come up short. Those are skipped
# by `complete` entirely: a type that cannot promise its full count every night must not be able to
# hold the refetch signal down forever.
#
# READ WHAT THAT MEANS ON THE CLIENT: a pack can arrive `complete: true` while missing an entire
# type, and no amount of refetching will fill it. Treat `complete: true` as "stop refetching", never
# as "every type is present" -- render whatever puzzles arrived and do not reserve a slot for a type
# that is not in the list. The backend does keep trying: whether a date is handed to the pack
# builder is a SEPARATE question from `complete` -- "is anything here still worth attempting" -- and
# a best-effort gap answers yes to it while `complete` ignores it. Of today's six types exactly one,
# crypticclue, is best-effort.
#
# A pack with `complete: false` holds real, playable puzzles and should be rendered; the client
# simply keeps refetching it.
#
# EVERY TYPE APPLIES TO EVERY DATE THIS ROUTE SERVES. All six declare 2026-01-01 as the first pack
# date they apply to, which is also PACK_START_DATE -- the lower bound isValidPackDate enforces -- so
# there is no date on the wire that predates a type and no transition date to special-case. A client
# needs no availability calendar; the counts below hold for every date that answers 200.
#
# A FULL pack is 16 puzzles and 2,415 seconds of estimated play. A COMPLETE one is 14 and 2,115, and
# that is not a contradiction. Cryptic Clue is BEST EFFORT: it is excluded from the `complete`
# calculation entirely, so a night that produced no clue is still a complete pack and a night that
# produced its full count ships a fifteenth and a sixteenth puzzle. THE GAP IS TWO PUZZLES, not one:
# this type owes two a day, so a pack reading `complete: true` may hold zero, one, or two cryptic
# clues. A client must not infer the pack is short from a missing crypticclue puzzle, and must not
# refetch over one.
#
#   gofigure        3, difficulties 2, 4, 5    420 s
#   missingvowels   3, difficulties 1, 2, 4    240 s
#   cryptogram      2, difficulties 2, 3       450 s
#   themedanagrams  3, difficulties 1, 3, 4    255 s
#   crypticclue     2, difficulties 3, 5       300 s   -- BEST EFFORT: a pack missing ONLY these
#                                                         puzzles is still `complete`
#   phrazle         3, difficulties 2, 3, 5    750 s
#
# ONE LINE PER TYPE, so a new type APPENDS a line rather than editing a sentence three ways. Every
# difficulty band appears. Only goFigure is built inside the request -- the phrase types need a
# phrase, and Themed Anagrams and Cryptic Clue each need a model call of their own.
#
# These are the SHIPPING numbers, and 16 puzzles / 2,415 seconds is the design's own count-table
# total for the six types now registered rather than a projection. Phase 2 types exist in the
# catalog; none is on the wire, and a client should read this list rather than that one. A date whose
# pack holds fewer than 14 puzzles is a date still being filled, not a date a type does not apply to.
#
# EVERY NUMBER IN THIS BLOCK IS DERIVED, and nothing in CI derives it. Each row is that type's
# PackContribution -- countPerDay, difficulties, and the per-band sum of
# `baseSeconds + secondsPerDifficulty * (difficulty - 1)` -- and the two totals are the column sums,
# with `complete` taking every row but the best-effort one. So a band moved in a contribution literal
# changes the truth here and cannot fail anything. That is exactly how this table came to disagree
# with the registry on ALL SIX types at once while staying perfectly self-consistent -- its rows
# summed to its own stated 13 puzzles and 1,935 seconds, so nothing about it read as broken; it was
# simply describing an older count table in full. Re-derive the whole block when any contribution
# changes; do not patch the one row that prompted the visit.
#
# Every puzzle carries id, type, difficulty, estimatedSeconds and data, and only `data` varies by
# type. (Listed by name, not in wire order -- on the wire it arrives data, difficulty,
# estimatedSeconds, id, type.)
#
#   `id`               `${date}:${type}:${short}`, where `short` is 8 hex characters. OPAQUE -- it
#                      carries no position and no difficulty. Do not parse it.
#   `type`             one of, and a new type is APPENDED here rather than edited into a line:
#                        "gofigure"
#                        "missingvowels"
#                        "cryptogram"
#                        "themedanagrams"
#                        "crypticclue"
#                        "phrazle"
#   `difficulty`       1-5, and WITHIN-TYPE: a 4 goFigure is hard for a goFigure and is not
#                      comparable to a 4 of another type.
#   `estimatedSeconds` derived from the type and the difficulty, and the one number that IS
#                      comparable across types. The UI PRINTS it on every row and does NOT sort on
#                      it -- lull-ui orders a day by difficulty, then bench, then id.
#
# Key order on the wire is JSON.stringify insertion order, and the examples below are written in
# the order they actually arrive. It carries no meaning; read every field by name.
#
# The per-type `data` shapes -- and the hint ladder, which is ONE shape wherever it appears but
# appears on only THREE of the six types and runs ONE TO THREE rungs -- are documented in the PUZZLE
# DATA SHAPES reference block at the bottom of this file.
# Read it once before writing a client; you do not need it to fire a request.
#
# 400 -> { "message": "Invalid pack date" } -- see the next block.
# 404 -> { "message": "No pack for date" } -- and ONLY when the pack ends up with zero puzzles. A
#      partial pack is a 200 with `complete: false`. The 404 is load-bearing rather than defensive:
#      a 200 carrying an empty `puzzles` array would be cached under today's date and would shadow
#      yesterday's good pack on the shelf, whereas a 404 caches nothing.
# 500 -> { "message": "Internal server error" }, the generic body from utils/status. Retryable.
#
# SIDE EFFECT: this GET WRITES. A request is proof the client is online, so a missing or partial
# pack is topped up from the generators graded fast enough to run in a request (today that is
# goFigure alone) while the request is open, under a 10-second budget inside the function's
# 15-second timeout.
#
# That budget stops work being STARTED; it cannot interrupt a generate() already running
# (services/packs.ts, ON_DEMAND_BUDGET_MS). It bounds how many puzzles get attempted, not worst-case
# latency -- do not size a client timeout from it.
#
# Anything still needing a model call is handed to the async builders once the PACK is assembled --
# but that hand-off is awaited BEFORE the response returns (claimPackGeneration then
# invokeSlowGenerators in handlers/get-pack-by-date.ts, both ahead of the return). It is a
# DynamoDB UpdateItem plus one Lambda invoke per builder, and all of them sit on your latency path. It never turns a serveable 200 into a 500. A pack that needs nothing costs one read and no
# write.
#
# Repeated calls do not stack up invocations: the hand-off is behind a TTL claim written onto the
# pack item (GenerationStarted, 900 seconds), so a date that cannot be completed is handed off once
# per window rather than on every app open. ONE claim covers every builder: the attribute means "an
# async build for this date is in flight", not "the phrase build is in flight".
#
# A top-up never REPLACES an existing puzzle, only adds the difficulties a pack is missing. Ids are
# stable while content is not, so regenerating wholesale would orphan the progress a player already
# stored against them.
#
# Throttled at the gateway: 25 req/s, burst 50, aggregate across all callers.
#
# The date below is a literal -- edit it to the day you want.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/packs/2026-08-20 HTTP/1.1

### Malformed or out-of-range date — 400
#
# The date is validated BEFORE the table is touched, because it is both a DynamoDB key and, now
# that this route writes, the only thing bounding which dates a caller can cause to be GENERATED.
#
# Four ways to earn this one body. The shape and calendar checks are `isPackDateFormat`
# (utils/pack-date.ts:19-32) and the range check is `isValidPackDate` (:35-36); the fourth is in the
# handler:
#   - wrong shape: anything not matching /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
#   - impossible calendar date: "2026-02-30" is not NaN, it rolls forward to March 2nd, and only the
#     round trip back through toPackDate catches that
#   - out of range: earlier than PACK_START_DATE (2026-01-01 in both environments), or later than
#     TOMORROW. The nightly schedule generates for the following UTC day, so tomorrow is the upper
#     bound of every date this API will ever serve. Both bounds are compared lexically, which for
#     YYYY-MM-DD is a chronological comparison.
#   - missing entirely: no `date` path parameter at all (handlers/get-pack-by-date.ts:19)
#
# 400 -> { "message": "Invalid pack date" } for all four. Do not try to tell them apart.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/packs/2026-02-30 HTTP/1.1

### A date with no stored pack — 200 incomplete
#
# EDIT THE DATE BELOW BEFORE RUNNING THIS. There is no date that can be hard-coded here and still
# demonstrate anything, which is the point of the block:
#
#   - Anything EARLIER than PACK_START_DATE (2026-01-01) is a 400, not a 404.
#   - Anything LATER than tomorrow is also a 400.
#   - WHICH ENVIRONMENT YOU ARE POINTED AT DECIDES THIS. The 03:33 UTC schedule -- the only one, now
#     that the 05:33 retry is removed -- is ENABLED in prod and DISABLED in test (template.yaml,
#     scheduledPackCreationState). Against prod, most in-range dates were generated overnight and
#     answer 200 with `complete: true` -- the ordinary case, not this one -- and that schedule only
#     ever targets tomorrow (handlers/create-pack.ts), so in-range dates from BEFORE the stack was
#     deployed are the exception and have no pack. Against test NOTHING is pre-generated: every date holds only what
#     on-demand fill and the hand invoke below have built, which makes this block the ordinary case
#     rather than the hard-to-stage one.
#   - PACK_START_DATE is 2026-01-01 and the upper bound is tomorrow, so the in-range window GROWS BY
#     ONE A DAY -- 250 dates on 2026-09-06 -- and the runbook deliberately does NOT bootstrap all of
#     them. An un-generated archive date is expected, not a fault. Compute the depth; do not read a
#     number off this file.
#
# Your best bet is TOMORROW before the 03:33 UTC run, or an in-range date predating the stack. Note
# that neither is guaranteed: this GET WRITES, and lull-ui's usePrefetch runs on open, on reconnect
# and on every resume, so a date may already have been filled by someone opening the app. It requests
# exactly ONE date, the one the shelf renders -- the fan-out is narrow and per-resume, not wide and
# per-open.
#
# 200 with `complete: false` is the expected answer, NOT a 404: this route writes, so the missing
# goFigure puzzles are built before it answers, and three of them come back while the OTHER FIVE
# TYPES arrive on a later fetch. Two async builders produce those five, not one: the phrase types
# need a phrase, and Themed Anagrams and Cryptic Clue each need a model call of their own.
#
# 404 -> { "message": "No pack for date" } is what is left when even that produces nothing -- a
# goFigure draw that failed, or a write that did not land. Rare, and still a response a client must
# handle: show the newest cached day, not an empty shelf under today's heading.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/packs/2026-08-21 HTTP/1.1


### Get the guess dictionary for a version

# GET /v1/dictionary/{version}
#
# THE SECOND ROUTED ENDPOINT, and the only large unauthenticated payload in this stack. Serves the
# word list the Phrazle board validates a player's guess against. Fetched ONCE per version by a
# device that intends to play Phrazle, and cached forever -- the version is in the URL, so the
# response is genuinely immutable.
#
# `{version}` is ALLOW-LISTED, never parsed and never interpolated into a path: it is compared
# against a frozen list before anything else happens. Today the list is ["v1"], and it holds at most
# two members -- a bump keeps the previous version served for one retention window so a device
# cached on the old one keeps working while its app updates.
#
# 200 -> the word list, gzipped
#        content-encoding: gzip
#        content-type: text/plain
#        cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# 400 -> { "message": "Invalid dictionary version" }
# 429 -> API Gateway's own throttle response, and A DESIGNED OUTCOME rather than an error.
#
# THE 429 IS THE ONE TO BUILD FOR. This route is throttled at 2 requests per second across ALL
# CALLERS -- HTTP API route throttling is per-route and per-stage, never per-IP and never per-client
# -- because its cost is EGRESS rather than a table read, and this route is the only large
# unauthenticated payload in the stack: 359,553 gzipped bytes per call, 479,404 base64, measured on
# the committed asset. THIS ROUTE IS CAPPED AT 2 rps, well under the stack default of 25, and that
# cap is the control. At 2 rps it is 83 GB/day of egress -- a CEILING rather than a forecast, since a
# device fetches once per version and caches. A device that gets a 429 has no dictionary and
# therefore no Phrazle board: show the type as DISABLED on the shelf with "needs a connection to set
# up", and RETRY WITH EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF AND FULL JITTER (base 1 s, factor 2, cap 60 s), across app
# opens rather than giving up. Without jitter a coordinated app update produces a synchronized retry
# storm, which is the same event one second later. The burst is 25 rather than 5 precisely so a
# cold-start stampede is absorbed rather than turned into that event; a stampede past it still
# degrades to the disabled shelf, which is honest, rather than to a broken board.
#
# THE LIST: every entry of the ENABLE word list that is TWO TO ELEVEN letters long -- 141,047 words,
# uppercase, one per line, sorted, ~350 KB gzipped. The length filter is PROVABLY LOSSLESS for this
# game rather than a trim of "uncommon" words: a guess word must match one of the answer's word
# lengths, and no answer word is outside that range, so nothing outside it can appear in a valid
# guess.
#
# IT WAS THREE TO SEVEN LETTERS, 51,852 WORDS AND ~123 KB, and it grew because the Phrazle board did.
# The answer floor went from 2-3 words of 3-7 letters to 2-6 words of 2-11, which is what admits
# KNOCK YOUR SOCKS OFF and PIECE OF THE ACTION -- and the dictionary is DERIVED from that floor, so
# it had to grow with it or the board would reject the player's own correct answer. The cost is one
# cached fetch, roughly 0.12 MB -> 0.34 MB; it is not paid per puzzle. A client caching the old
# list MUST refetch: a two-letter word like OF is in no previously served version.
#
# Membership is CASE-SENSITIVE and expects the canonical form -- uppercase A-Z -- which is the same
# form `answer` ships in.
#
# It is a MEMBERSHIP ORACLE and never a display corpus: it accepts words no puzzle would ever show,
# and it holds no proper nouns, which is why a Phrazle answer never contains one.
#
# EVERY GENERATED ANSWER IS VALIDATED AGAINST v1 AND EVERY LATER VERSION IS A SUPERSET, so a client
# on any served version holds every word of every answer it will ever be given. That is what makes
# the packs-first/dictionary-last prefetch order safe.
#
# Delete any cached version that is not the current one.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/dictionary/v1 HTTP/1.1

