### NOT AN HTTP REQUEST — building a pack for a MISSING date by hand
###
### RECORDED, NOT EXECUTABLE. REST Client would try to parse the shell lines below as a request, so
### they stay inside this comment. Run them in a terminal.
###
### This is the README bootstrap invoke, and it is how a missing date gets a pack by hand -- a fresh
### stack serves an empty app until the next 03:33 UTC and nothing tells you, because
### createPackHandler returns normally when there is nothing to do and the level="ERROR" log
### subscription therefore fires nothing.
###
### IN TEST THERE IS NO NEXT 03:33: scheduledPackCreationState is DISABLED there, so this invoke and
### on-demand fill are the ONLY things that ever build a pack in that environment. "Wait for the
### nightly" is prod-only advice.
###
### template.yaml sets no FunctionName on CreatePackFunction, so CloudFormation generates one with a
### stack suffix -- `lull-api-CreatePackFunction-<suffix>`. A bare `--function-name
### lull-api-CreatePackFunction` returns ResourceNotFoundException. Look the real name up first:
###
###   FN=$(aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource --stack-name lull-api \
###     --logical-resource-id CreatePackFunction \
###     --query 'StackResourceDetail.PhysicalResourceId' --output text)
###
###   for d in $(date -u +%F) $(date -u -v+1d +%F); do
###     aws lambda invoke --function-name "$FN" \
###       --payload "$(printf '{"date":"%s"}' "$d")" --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out \
###       /dev/stdout
###   done
###
### It TOPS UP; it never replaces. createPack generates only the difficulties a pack is missing, so
### re-running it against a date that already has three goFigures adds nothing and is safe to repeat.
### It builds the self-contained puzzles and hands everything that needs a model call to the async
### builders, through the one fan-out in services/lambda.ts.
###
### That is SIMILAR to the request path, not identical: createPack runs every self-contained
### generator with no time budget, while the GET path (fillPack) filters on `inRequest` and applies
### the 10-second budget. Both are in services/packs.ts. They coincide only because goFigure is the
### sole self-contained generator today.
###
### That also means it is NOT a way to rebuild a pack that already exists. Rebuilding is a DEPLOY,
### then a delete, then this -- in that order, which is not the order this list carried until
### 2026-09-07:
###
###   0. SHIP THE lull-ui READER FIRST, WHATEVER THE SHAPE CHANGE IS. Not optional and not
###      reorderable. The rule has two halves and both bind: this API must not emit a shape the
###      client has not learned, and it must not STOP emitting one the client still requires. Both
###      halves have now been exercised, so the step is stated as the rule with its instances under
###      it rather than as the one case that first needed it.
###
###        * A FIELD ADDED -- the case this step was written for. GoFigureHintMetadata gained a
###          required `kind`, and a pack carrying it reaches a client with no name for the tag.
###        * A FIELD REMOVED -- 2026-08-31, when cryptogram, phrazle and themedanagrams stopped
###          shipping `hints`. A pack WITHOUT it reaches a client that still calls hintsOf, gets
###          null, and draws no hint bar at all on three of its six benches. All three types are live
###          from PACK_START_DATE, so there is no date on which the old client is safe.
###
###      A REMOVAL CAN GO FIRST ON THE CLIENT WITH NO GAP TO COVER, which is what makes this cheap
###      rather than awkward: the device computes those rungs from `answer`, already on the wire and
###      not changing, so a client can stop reading pack hints well before this API stops sending
###      them. During the gap both are true at once and nothing reads the field.
###
###   THE NUMBERS ARE THE RUN ORDER. They were not, until 2026-09-07: the delete used to be step 1
###   and the deploy step 2, and running them in that order permanently re-poisons the table. See
###   THE DELETE GOES AFTER THE DEPLOY below for the trace. Do not re-sort these.
###
###   1. Deploy lull-api. NOT AN OPERATOR ACTION: merging this branch does it, unasked and
###      unapproved. See MERGING THIS BRANCH DEPLOYS IT below.
###   2. Delete every pack item from the packs table by hand. ONLY WHEN A STORED PACK HAS BECOME
###      UNREADABLE TO THE NEW CLIENT -- see clause (b) below, and read that before running this.
###
###      THE 2026-09-07 CRYPTIC CLUE CHANGE IS A LIVE INSTANCE OF THAT CONDITION, and the first one
###      this runbook has had. It is REQUIRED for that deploy rather than optional, and the reason is
###      the device set rather than the shape: every stored crypticclue puzzle is "hidden" or
###      "anagram", those were the only two values the union ever held, and both were deleted. There
###      is no surviving valid cryptic puzzle to preserve, so there is nothing to weigh -- the usual
###      argument for sparing the archive does not apply because the archive holds no readable
###      cryptic. availableFrom is 2026-01-01, so that is roughly eight months of packs.
###
###      Nothing after this point may read a
###      puzzle built before the shape change. Nothing automates it and nothing ever will by
###      accident: the table carries Retain on both delete and replace, createPack tops up rather
###      than replaces, and no Lambda role holds a delete action on it -- every DynamoDB grant in
###      template.yaml is a hand-written list of BatchGetItem, GetItem and UpdateItem, with neither
###      DeleteItem nor BatchWriteItem anywhere in the file. So this runs under an operator's own
###      credentials or it does not run.
###
###      THE COMMANDS, because "by hand" is not a procedure and this is the one irreversible step in
###      the list. Three things have to be derived before the obvious attempt even parses, and the
###      obvious attempt does not parse:
###
###        * THE TABLE NAME is lull-api-packs in production and lull-api-packs-test in testing --
###          template.yaml's EnvironmentMap, `packsTable` under `prod` and under `test`, resolved
###          into PacksTable's `TableName: !FindInMap [...]`. There is no stack suffix on this one,
###          unlike the Lambda names above it.
###        * THE KEY IS `Date`, a single String HASH key and the whole of the KeySchema. One
###          delete-item call per date is therefore the complete operation.
###        * `Date` IS A DYNAMODB RESERVED WORD, which this repo already has to work around twice --
###          getPackDates aliases it in a ProjectionExpression and setPackByDate aliases it in a
###          ConditionExpression, both in services/dynamodb.ts, both with the comment saying a bare
###          `Date` is a runtime ValidationException no mocked unit test would ever see. So the first
###          thing anyone types fails:
###
###            aws dynamodb scan --table-name lull-api-packs --projection-expression Date
###            -> ValidationException: Invalid ProjectionExpression: Attribute name is a reserved
###               keyword; reserved keyword: Date
###
###          The alias is needed in EXPRESSIONS only. `--key` below is an Item, not an expression, so
###          the bare `Date` there is correct and is not an oversight.
###
###      INSPECT FIRST. This writes the date list to a file rather than piping it into the delete,
###      deliberately: the list is the thing to read before anything is destroyed, and it is also
###      what makes the delete resumable if it is interrupted halfway.
###
###        aws dynamodb scan --table-name lull-api-packs \
###          --projection-expression '#d' --expression-attribute-names '{"#d":"Date"}' \
###          --query 'Items[].Date.S' --output text | tr '\t' '\n' | sort > /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###        wc -l < /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###        head -3 /tmp/pack-dates.txt; tail -3 /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###
###      A SHORT LIST HERE IS THE DANGEROUS FAILURE, and it is silent. Scan's 1MB page limit counts
###      bytes read FROM THE TABLE, before the projection applies, and a pack measures 12,441 bytes
###      (__tests__/unit/services/packs-size.test.ts) -- so ONE PAGE IS ABOUT 84 DATES against an
###      archive of ~250. The AWS CLI v2 follows LastEvaluatedKey itself, which is the only reason
###      the command above is one line; --no-paginate, --max-items, or a v1 CLI silently reads the
###      first page and the delete then clears a third of the table and reports success. Check the
###      count against (today - 2026-01-01) + 2 before continuing. DO NOT use `describe-table
###      --query 'Table.ItemCount'` for that check: it is updated roughly every six hours and is
###      documented as approximate.
###
###      THEN DELETE. One call per date, not BatchWriteItem: 250 sequential calls take about a
###      minute, each one is independently idempotent, and re-running the loop after an interruption
###      is safe -- deleting an absent key succeeds.
###
###        while read -r d; do
###          aws dynamodb delete-item --table-name lull-api-packs \
###            --key "$(printf '{"Date":{"S":"%s"}}' "$d")"
###        done < /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###
###      THEN CONFIRM IT IS EMPTY.
###
###        aws dynamodb scan --table-name lull-api-packs --select COUNT --query 'Count' --output text
###
###      COUNT is read here and NOT in the inspect step above, and the asymmetry is the pagination
###      hazard again: the only answer this has to distinguish is zero from non-zero, and an empty
###      table is one page. A non-zero answer means either the read was short or a client rebuilt a
###      date while the loop ran -- both are fine and both are fixed by re-running the inspect and
###      the loop.
###
###      WHAT STANDS BETWEEN A TYPO AND THE ARCHIVE: PointInTimeRecoveryEnabled is true on this
###      table, so its pre-delete state can be restored for 35 days -- to a NEW TABLE, which is the
###      part that matters. It is not an undo. The live table keeps its name and its emptiness, and
###      putting the rows back means a restore plus a copy, so treat it as forensics rather than as a
###      safety net.
###   3. Re-run the bootstrap invoke above for TODAY AND TOMORROW only, serially. Everything older
###      fills on request, which is the deliberate design: PACK_START_DATE is 2026-01-01, so the
###      servable window is 250 dates today and one more tomorrow, and bootstrapping all of them
###      would be ~1,250 Opus calls in one sitting to pre-build an archive nobody has asked for.
###      FIVE CALLS PER DATE, not two: create-phrases, review-phrases, create-anagram-sets,
###      create-cryptic-clues and review-cryptic-clues, one prompt each under prompts/.
###
###      An earlier version of this step said EVERY DATE from PACK_START_DATE, and it was right for
###      the window it was written against -- 24 dates, when leaving them empty meant a cold client
###      hit empty packs. That argument does not survive a 250-date window: pre-building it costs an
###      order of magnitude more than the fan-out it prevents, and the fan-out is bounded anyway --
###      claimPackGeneration is per date per TTL, and usePrefetch requests exactly ONE date per
###      resume, so the repair rate is one claim per date somebody actually asked for.
###
###      What you accept by doing it this way: a request for an un-generated archive date answers
###      200 with the self-contained puzzles built inside the request, complete: false, and the
###      phrase and model types arriving on a later fetch. That is the normal cold-date path, not a
###      degraded one.
###
###   4. CHECK THE SHAPE OF WHAT IS NOW STORED, and there is a command for it rather than an
###      instruction to look:
###
###        npm run audit-cryptic -- lull-api-packs --days 40 --no-model
###
###      It reports `stale N over M dates` -- puzzles and dates -- by checking each stored cryptic
###      for `explanation`, the field that replaced definitionSpan/fodderSpan/device on 2026-09-07
###      and therefore the only thing that dates a stored puzzle. --no-model spends no Bedrock
###      tokens: the stale check runs before the model branch, so this is a shape read and nothing
###      else. THE TABLE NAME IS REQUIRED, for the reason step 5 gives.
###
###      WHAT IT PROVES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. --days is capped at 40 and the window always ends
###      tomorrow, so this reads the most recent 40 dates and says nothing about the other ~210. That
###      is the right coverage AFTER step 2 -- the archive is empty and only the rebuilt dates exist
###      -- and it is the wrong coverage for anything else. The archive-wide check is step 2's own
###      `--select COUNT` returning 0; this one checks that what step 3 rebuilt is the new shape.
###      Anything other than `stale 0 over 0 dates` here means step 2 missed rows or something wrote
###      after it.
###
###      THEN fetch a live pack date and assert `kind` is present on every goFigure rung. Skipping
###      this leaves a runtime `undefined` behind a non-optional field -- the exact case read sites
###      were told not to branch on. Nothing automates that one; audit-cryptic reads cryptics only.
###   5. `npm run audit-hints -- lull-api-packs --days 2` to confirm the audit still reads the new
###      shape. THE TABLE NAME IS REQUIRED; a bare run reads the test table, and a clean audit of the
###      test table says nothing about production.
###   6. Confirm the new alarms in the logs, and confirm THE FILTER READS THEM. After the first
###      nightly run, check that `stopReason` appears on the model-invocation lines and that no
###      max_tokens ERROR fired -- that is the only reading anyone gets on whether 32,000 tokens was
###      enough. IN THE SAME PASS, confirm that a real logError line actually matches
###      '[timestamp, uuid, level="ERROR", message]'. That filter is POSITIONAL, not a substring
###      search; FIVE subscriptions in template.yaml use it and two shipping conditions assume it
###      works (the per-type logError({ type }) on both lanes, and the stop_reason promotion);
###      and nothing in this repo has ever checked that logError's emitted shape satisfies it. One
###      log-group query. NOT bestEffort's type-level line, which is `log` and not `logError` and so
###      can never match a level="ERROR" filter -- and note the per-type lines drop to logWarning
###      when upstream is unavailable, so a transient outage produces WARN rather than ERROR.
###
### Nothing else is migrated and nothing backfills content. A PLAYED DAY DOES GET A REBALANCED PACK,
### and that is deliberate: the argument for leaving played days alone holds while the archive still
### HAS those packs, and does not survive the delete removing them. The choice is then between a
### rebuilt pack and no pack at all, and no pack is what the fan-out feeds on. Player progress is
### keyed to puzzle ids the delete already destroyed, so nothing extra is lost by rebuilding.
###
### One visible consequence, named so nobody debugs it as a bug: after every deploy, each cached pack
### in the client's retention window reports incomplete once and the window refetches.
###
### EXPECT A LOT OF FAN-OUT AFTER THIS PARTICULAR DEPLOY, and the sentence that used to end this
### paragraph said the opposite: "after step 3 there should be no genuinely short date left to fan
### out at all". That is true of a deploy that deletes nothing. It is false here by construction --
### step 2 empties ~250 dates and step 3 rebuilds TWO, so every other servable date is genuinely
### short until somebody asks for it, which is the deliberate trade step 3 spells out. The fan-out is
### the design working, not a symptom: one claim per date per TTL, one date per resume, and a cold
### date answers 200 with its self-contained puzzles built inside the request.
###
### DO NOT expect availableFrom to hold that line either: all six types declare 2026-01-01, which is
### PACK_START_DATE, so appliesTo is true on every servable date and availableFrom filters nothing
### today. It is a guard for a type added later, not a live suppressor -- see the block below.
###
### MERGING THIS BRANCH DEPLOYS IT, AND NOBODY IS ASKED FIRST. .github/workflows/pipeline.yaml is
### `on: push` (:17), and `deploy-prod` is gated on `github.ref == 'refs/heads/master'` and
### `needs: [deploy-testing]` (:263-266) and nothing else -- there is no `environment:` key anywhere
### in that file, so no manual approval and no protection rule stands between a merge and production.
### The operator does not pick the moment of the deploy; they pick the moment of the delete, and the
### only ordering decision available is which side of the merge that falls on.
###
### THE DELETE GOES AFTER THE DEPLOY. This block said the reverse until 2026-09-07 -- "STEP 1 MUST BE
### RUN BY HAND IN THE WINDOW BEFORE THE MERGE" -- and the reverse re-poisons the table permanently.
### Trace it: between the delete and deploy-prod finishing, THE OLD CODE IS STILL LIVE. Every emptied
### date now holds zero cryptics, so hasWorkRemaining reports true where it reported false, every GET
### for one of those dates takes a claimPackGeneration and fans out, and CreateModelPuzzlesFunction
### -- still running the old build -- writes FRESH `hidden` and `anagram` cryptics into the dates the
### delete just cleared. In prod the 03:33 nightly does it unasked. Those puzzles then survive the
### deploy: the new code tops up rather than replaces, and a date holding two cryptics grades
### satisfied on a COUNT, so it is never handed to a builder again. The delete would have to be run a
### second time to undo what the first one caused, and nothing tells you it happened.
###
### The other order costs a bounded window in which old-shape packs are served to the new client --
### which is not a new hazard. It is the hazard the delete exists to END, and it has already been
### running for as long as the archive has existed. Nothing writes an old-shape puzzle once the new
### code is live, so the delete is monotone: what it clears stays cleared, and a date rebuilt on
### demand two minutes later is rebuilt by the new generator.
###
### THERE IS NO GUARD THAT WOULD MAKE THE OLD ORDER SAFE, which is why this is an ordering change and
### not a new safeguard. Stopping the old code from refilling an emptied date means disabling the
### 03:33 schedule or the request path's fan-out, and both of those are template.yaml or src/ changes
### -- a DEPLOY. Guarding a delete that must precede a deploy with a deploy is circular. Reversing the
### two costs nothing and closes it completely.
###
### WHAT THE DELETE COSTS, stated honestly because two comments in this repo disagree about it. This
### block treats it as a routine precondition that step 3 undoes. The PacksTable comment in
### template.yaml says the opposite about the same table -- a deleted historical pack "is not
### guaranteed to come back identical, and the ids a player's progress is keyed to would not survive
### it", and "a stack delete or an accidental table replacement would destroy the entire archive with
### no way back" -- which is why that table carries Retain on both delete and replace. Step 2 is a
### hand-run version of exactly that destruction: it removes the ENTIRE archive, not only the dates
### step 3 rebuilds, and every puzzle id in it is regenerated rather than restored. (No line numbers:
### the block has moved once already and a stale citation reads as a checked one. Search PacksTable.)
###
### THE PRICE, PRICED. The archive is at most (today - PACK_START_DATE) + 1 days deep -- 250 days on
### 2026-09-06, growing by one a day -- and this is pre-launch. So the delete costs regenerated
### historical puzzles, which are
### different puzzles rather than lost ones, plus any client-side lull:progress keyed to the old ids,
### which is orphaned rather than migrated. That is cheap NOW and expensive after launch, and it is
### the argument for landing the wire change early rather than deferring it behind a game branch.
### The price is accepted; what is not negotiable is the ORDER, because a delete run on the wrong
### side of the merge writes NEW bad rows that no later step in this list removes.
###
### NOTHING AUTOMATES THIS, and nothing ever will by accident. template.yaml sets DeletionPolicy and
### UpdateReplacePolicy to Retain on the packs table; no Lambda role holds a delete action on it --
### every role is a hand-scoped statement, none is a managed policy template, and that is already
### true in production rather than pending a deploy; and createPack tops up rather than replaces,
### so a pack written under an old shape
### survives every deploy, indefinitely, serving old-shape puzzles to a client built for new ones.
