### WHICH KIND OF CHANGE YOU HAVE, since that is the whole of what decides it:
###
###   (a) AN ADDITIVE TYPE -- a new PuzzleType literal, a new HintMetadata member, a new `data` shape,
###       and no change to a shape already on the wire. Ship the lull-ui reader first, then merge.
###       NO WIPE: every stored pack keeps exactly the puzzles it has, and nothing rewrites a date
###       that predates the type. Themed Anagrams, Cryptic Clue and Phrazle were all this case.
###
###       THE GATE IS THE DEPLOY ORDER, NOT availableFrom, and the three types above are why this
###       reads differently than it once did. Staging availableFrom to the day after the reader
###       ships buys nothing the ordering does not already buy -- the reader is out before the type
###       can produce anything either way -- while it costs a date every stored pack has to be
###       reasoned about against, and a literal that silently applies to NO date if it is written
###       unpadded. All six types now declare 2026-01-01, which is PACK_START_DATE, so the registry
###       is the same on every servable date. Set a new type's availableFrom to PACK_START_DATE too,
###       and hold the type back by not merging it.
###
###       What availableFrom is still FOR: it is read by isComplete when a pack is regraded, so it
###       remains the thing that would stop a newly-shipped type marking the entire archive
###       incomplete at once. That guard is live and worth keeping; it simply is not the release
###       lever.
###
###   (b) A SHAPE CHANGE TO AN EXISTING TYPE -- any field added, removed or retyped on a `data` or a
###       `hints` payload that stored packs already carry. STEP 0 ALWAYS AND WITHOUT EXCEPTION: the
###       lull-ui reader ships first and it is not reorderable.
###
###       STEPS 1-6 ARE NOT AUTOMATIC, and reading "(b)" as "the whole runbook" is the mistake this
###       clause was rewritten to stop. They exist for a change that leaves a STORED pack unreadable
###       to the new client -- a field whose absence the new client cannot tolerate, or a value it
###       would misread. Ask that question before running any of them: if every stored pack is still
###       serviceable to the NEW client, step 2 deletes the archive for nothing and steps 3-6 repair
###       a wound nobody inflicted. The 2026-08-31 hint-field removal is the worked example and it
###       took step 0 alone, for the reason listed above.
###
###       WHEN THEY DO APPLY, the delete (step 2) is run BY HAND AFTER the merge has finished
###       deploying, never in the window before it -- see THE DELETE GOES AFTER THE DEPLOY above for
###       the trace, and note that this instruction was the other way round until 2026-09-07.
###
###       AND THE QUESTION "IS EVERY STORED PACK STILL SERVICEABLE" HAS AN INSTRUMENT ON EXACTLY ONE
###       TYPE. `npm run audit-cryptic -- <table> --days 40 --no-model` answers it for Cryptic Clue by
###       checking each stored puzzle for `explanation`. There is no equivalent for the other five,
###       and adding one is the honest response to the next (b) change rather than reading a cryptic
###       audit as an all-clear for a pack.
###
### THE DETECTION STORY IS STILL THIN, and that is why (b) is a runbook rather than a deploy. Nothing
### validates a stored pack on read -- services/dynamodb.ts casts the parsed JSON straight to `Pack` --
### and no script, CI step or smoke check inspects `kind` anywhere. A stale-shape pack is served
### silently and the first symptom appears in lull-ui.
###
### ONE TYPE NOW HAS A DETECTOR, AND ONLY BY HAND. scripts/audit-cryptic.ts checks each stored cryptic
### for `explanation` and reports `stale N over M dates`; --no-model makes it a shape read that costs
### nothing. It is a script an operator runs, not a guard on the read path, and the difference is the
### whole of what is still thin: nothing runs it on a schedule, nothing fails a deploy over it, and it
### covers one of six types over at most 40 dates.
###
### WHY THE GUARD IS NOT ON THE READ PATH, since that is the obvious place to want it and it is not a
### free move. Validating in services/dynamodb.ts means a per-type schema for a six-way union, and
### then a decision about what to DO with a puzzle that fails -- and DROPPING one is the trap.
### buildPack passes `existingPuzzles.length` to setPackByDate as its ConditionExpression's
### `:expectedPuzzleCount`, checked against the PuzzleCount ATTRIBUTE written beside the blob. A read
### that silently dropped a puzzle would hand the write a count lower than the stored one, so the
### condition would fail on every subsequent write to that date and the date could never be repaired
### by anything -- a stale pack turned into a frozen one. Validating and THROWING is worse: it turns a
### stale pack into a 500 for a puzzle the player could otherwise still play. Both are real changes to
### the write protocol, and neither belongs in a migration.
