### REFERENCE — PUZZLE DATA SHAPES
###
### RECORDED, NOT EXECUTABLE. Everything below is the `data` payload of the three puzzle types, as
### returned by GET /v1/packs/{date} above. Read once before writing a client.
###
### THE HINT LADDER, WHICH THREE OF THE SIX TYPES CARRY
###
### THREE TYPES SHIP A LADDER AND THREE DO NOT, and that is the first thing to know before reading
### anything below. `hints` is present on gofigure, missingvowels and crypticclue. It is ABSENT --
### not null, not empty -- on cryptogram, phrazle and themedanagrams, as of 2026-08-31.
###
### WHY THOSE THREE STOPPED. Their players are doing LETTER work: filling a cipher, ruling letters in
### and out of a phrase, placing letters in a scrambled word. A useful rung there has to know what the
### player has already established -- which squares they have solved, which letters their guesses have
### eliminated, which of four words they have already got -- and none of that exists when a generator
### runs the night before. A ladder fixed before the player exists can only describe the puzzle, which
### is what those three ladders did, and it is why they were replaced rather than reworded. The device
### computes them now, from `answer`, which those types already ship in plaintext. The builders are
### NOT shared with this repo: they live only in lull-ui, at src/components/{cryptogram,phrazle,
### themedanagrams}/rungs.ts. src/rules/ holds exactly three files -- normalize-answer.ts,
### is-valid-guess.ts, mark-guess.ts -- and no hint builder among them.
###
### A CLIENT MUST NOT REQUIRE `hints`. Read the key defensively: absent is a legitimate, permanent
### shape for those three types and not a degraded pack.
###
### On the three that DO carry it, `hints` is ONE TO THREE rungs, ORDERED BY THE BACKEND, and every
### rung is
###
###   { "metadata"?: { ... }, "text": "..." }
###
### READ `hints.length`. DO NOT ASSUME THREE. This changed on 2026-08-24: the array is one to three
### rungs and a client that indexes `hints[2]` blind will read `undefined` on a short ladder. Today
### only crypticclue ever ships fewer than three -- gofigure and missingvowels still ship exactly
### three -- but the ARRAY is the contract, not the count, and a type that shortens later must not be
### a client change.
###
### WHY A LADDER MAY BE SHORT: a rung is dropped when the puzzle already says what it would say.
### There are exactly two such rules on Cryptic Clue -- the gloss drops when the model supplied none
### or the content gates rejected it, and the definition rung drops when the definition is a single
### word, which is a word the player is already looking at. Padding the ladder back to three meant
### emitting a second letter reveal beside the first, which is the same hint twice. A rung the backend
### does not have is better than a bad one, so it ships what it has.
###
### A DELETION'S DEVICE RUNG IS NOT DROPPED; IT WAS NEVER DECLARED. The indicator announces its own
### operation -- "endless", "beheaded", "heartless" all state what happens to the letters -- on every
### deletion clue without exception, so a sentence naming the device would hand back a word already on
### the player's screen every time. A rung declared with a drop rule that fires 100% of the time is a
### rung the pool pretends to have, so the deletion pool simply has four entries where the charade
### pool has five.
###
### That reasoning was first written for the retired hidden/anagram devices and it survived the
### 2026-09-07 device change intact, which is worth saying: the rule is about a rung restating the
### puzzle, not about any particular device.
###
### RENDER THEM IN THE ORDER THEY ARRIVE. Do not sort them and do not renumber them. The order is NOT
### always least-to-most-revealing: Missing Vowels does run that way, but a goFigure puzzle with a
### unique operator tuple deliberately spends rung 1 on op2, so its slots arrive 1, 0, 2. A client
### that re-sorts on an assumed ordering gets those puzzles wrong.
###
### `text` is the sentence, and it is RENDERED VERBATIM -- do not rewrite it, do not compose your own
### from the fields beside it, and do not translate it. For goFigure the backend writes it from a
### closed set of templates. For Missing Vowels it is model-written prose that passed the content
### gates in utils/phrase-checks.ts (the utils/charged-terms.ts blocklist, answer-leak check,
### 200-character cap). Cryptic Clue is NEITHER: it has THREE POOLS, one per device -- five entries
### on a charade, four on a deletion, three on a double definition, eight distinct rungs between them
### -- and every one of them but the gloss is composed in code. One quotes a slice of the clue (the
### definition), two are fixed sentences naming the device, three interpolate the part or source
### letters the verifier proved, and one names the answer's first letter. Only the gloss is model
### prose -- gated by passesStringGates in utils/model-output-checks.ts at an 80-character cap, with a
### 145-character cap over every emitted rung. Decided here, but not synthesized here, and worth
### knowing if you render into anything other than a text node.
### `metadata` is
### machine-readable structure for the board, present only where the type has any: goFigure has it,
### Missing Vowels and Cryptic Clue do not. A renderer that only prints `hint.text` works on all
### three types today and on every type added later.
###
### 2026-08-31 -- THREE TYPES LOST `hints`. A FIELD REMOVAL, WITH A DEPLOY ORDER, AND NO REBUILD.
###
### THE DEPLOY ORDER IS AN OPERATIONAL FACT AND IT IS NOT WRITTEN HERE. It lives in the runbook
### above, in the paragraph beginning "ONE SHIPPING CHANGE REQUIRES PART OF THIS RUNBOOK", which
### names step 0 as the only step this change takes and the delete (step 2) as the one it must never
### run. This
### block is REFERENCE for someone writing a client, and it used to carry its own summary of the
### ordering -- which is how the two came to contradict each other, one saying no pack is deleted and
### the other citing, four lines later, a runbook one of whose steps deletes every pack. One copy.
###
### WHAT A CLIENT AUTHOR NEEDS FROM IT, AND IT IS ONE SENTENCE: a pack on one of those three types
### MAY STILL CARRY `hints`, and a client must ignore the key there. That is true during the gap
### between the two deploys and for as long afterwards as any device keeps a pack cached from before
### them. The field is not read, not rendered, and not a signal about how old the pack is.
###
### The shape before 2026-08-24 -- phrase puzzles shipping three plain strings, goFigure shipping
