### below holds for both. A NEW PHRASE-BACKED TYPE APPENDS A LINE to the list immediately below and
### changes nothing else; a type with a `data` shape of its own opens its own block instead.
###
### THEY NO LONGER AGREE ABOUT HINTS, and that is the one rule in this block that does not hold for
### both. Missing Vowels ships a prose ladder; Cryptogram ships none at all:
###
###   missingvowels -> { "answer", "category"?, "displayed",
###                      "hints": [{ "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }] }
###   cryptogram    -> { "answer", "category"?, "ciphertext" }
###
### No `metadata` key on Missing Vowels' rungs -- absent, not null. A phrase rung is a sentence; there
### is no structure on it for a board to act on.
###
### WHY CRYPTOGRAM LEFT AND MISSING VOWELS STAYED, since they draw from one corpus and this is the
### block that exists to say they are the same puzzle twice. The prose ladder is SEMANTIC by rule --
### prompts/create-phrases.txt forbids a rung about how the phrase is written -- and that is exactly
### right for Missing Vowels, where the player is recovering a phrase from its consonants and the work
### is recognition. A Cryptogram player is solving a substitution cipher one letter at a time, and a
### nudge about what the phrase MEANS is a hint aimed at the other costume. Its rungs are now computed
### on the device, against the squares the player has actually filled.
###
### THE CORPUS IS UNCHANGED. A phrase still needs three prose hints to be usable at all, because
### Missing Vowels still needs them; they simply stop reaching the wire for Cryptogram.
###
### `answer` ships for the same offline-first reason `acceptedSolutions` does.
###
### `category` is OMITTED, not null, wherever it is hidden -- hiding it is part of the difficulty.
### The table hides it at difficulties 3 AND 5 (generators/category-visibility.ts), and BOTH ROWS ARE
### NOW LIVE. The forward-looking "today only difficulty 3 ever loses it" clause that stood here is
### STRUCK: Phrazle declares [3, 5], which is the first phrase type to reach band 5, so row 5 is a
### shape on the wire rather than a rule waiting for a type.
###
### What that means per type, since it falls out of the band sets rather than the table: Missing
### Vowels runs [1, 2, 4] and always carries a category; Cryptogram runs [2, 3] and loses it at 3;
### and Phrazle runs [2, 3, 5], so it carries a category on its band-2 puzzle and loses it on the
### other two.
###
### THREE OF THOSE BAND LISTS HAVE MOVED SINCE THIS PARAGRAPH WAS FIRST WRITTEN, which is why it now
### reads as a derivation rather than as a claim. It used to say Cryptogram ran [3, 4] and that
### "PHRAZLE RUNS [3, 5], SO IT NEVER CARRIES A CATEGORY ON EITHER OF ITS PUZZLES" -- true when both
### lists happened to agree with the visibility table, and false the moment either moved. READ THE
### KEY; do not derive its presence from a band list quoted anywhere, this one included. The
