### --- themedanagrams ---
###
### One theme and FOUR scrambled words, shown together and adjudicated PER WORD on the device. There
### is no top-level `answer` and there is no `category`, at any difficulty -- this type has four
### strings the player types, not one, and its label is `theme`.
###
###   { "data": {
###       "entries": [
###         { "answer": "KETTLE",   "scrambles": ["ELETKT", "TLTEEK", "LTETEK", "TKEELT"] },
###         { "answer": "SAUCEPAN", "scrambles": ["NSAPUACE", "PACNSAEU", "NSCEAUPA", "UNASPCAE"] },
###         { "answer": "SKILLET",  "scrambles": ["TILKELS", "KTESLLI", "LLKSETI", "KSLLTIE"] },
###         { "answer": "SPATULA",  "scrambles": ["AAUPLTS", "PALSTUA", "TUPSLAA", "PSTUAAL"] }
###       ],
###       "theme": "Kitchen tools" },
###     "difficulty": 3,
###     "estimatedSeconds": 90,
###     "id": "2026-08-20:themedanagrams:abc123de",
###     "type": "themedanagrams" }
###
### THE EXAMPLE'S WIRE ORDER IS DELIBERATELY NOT LENGTH-SORTED, so nothing about it suggests that a
### rung's position tells you which row it is about. Ranked by answer length it is SAUCEPAN, then
### SKILLET, then SPATULA (the tie broken by wire position), then KETTLE -- which is why the ordinals
### come out 3rd, 4th, 2nd and why KETTLE, the shortest, is never named.
###
### `entries` IS EXACTLY FOUR, and it is RENDERED IN WIRE ORDER. The rung ordinals index that order,
### so a board that sorts entries by length -- the obvious tidy-up -- makes every rung point at the
### wrong row. Do not sort them and do not renumber them.
###
### `answer` and `scrambles` arrive as ONE OBJECT PER ENTRY, never as two parallel arrays. `answer` is
### uppercase A-Z, 6 to 9 letters, and every member of `scrambles` is always the same length and the
### same letters as its own answer -- proved in code before the puzzle is written, so a board may rely
### on it.
###
### `scrambles` IS A LIST OF 1 TO 4, AND YOU MUST READ ITS LENGTH. `scrambles[0]` is the board as it
### first appears; the rest are what a reshuffle control cycles through, IN THE ORDER GIVEN. The
### example above is a band-3 puzzle where every entry happened to get four. That is common and it is
### NOT a guarantee:
###
###   * The length varies PER ENTRY. Two entries in the same puzzle can hold four and one.
###   * ONE IS A NORMAL LENGTH, not an error and not a degenerate pack. It is a property of the word
###     at that difficulty: KETTLE at band 4 has exactly ONE arrangement of its letters that is hard
###     enough to show, out of 180. A board that assumes a second one is a board that breaks on a
###     perfectly good puzzle.
###   * A reshuffle control SHOULD HIDE OR DISABLE ITSELF when the length is 1. There is nothing to
###     cycle to, and a button that visibly does nothing reads as a bug in the app.
###   * There is never a 0. The field cannot be empty -- a word that drew nothing is SKIPPED and the
###     next word in the set is tried, so an empty entry never reaches the wire. Only a set that
###     cannot yield four such words drops that one difficulty for that set.
###
### DO NOT SORT, DEDUPE, RE-SHUFFLE OR EXTEND THIS LIST. Every member is separated from every other by
### a rule applied here -- at most a third of the tiles may sit in the same position in any two of
### them -- so cycling in the order given is what makes each press visibly move the board. A client
### that generates its own extra arrangement is generating one that has passed NO gate: not the
### difficulty dial, not the charged-string check below. Cycle back to `scrambles[0]` after the last
### one rather than inventing a fifth.
###
### THE ORDER CARRIES NO DIFFICULTY GRADIENT. Every member clears the same bar; `scrambles[0]` is not
### the easiest and the last is not the hardest. Do not present them as a ladder.
###
### NEITHER `answer` NOR ANY MEMBER OF `scrambles` IS A CHARGED TERM, and both halves of that are
### checked here rather than left to a client. The answer passes the whole-token blocklist in
### utils/charged-terms.ts, and its letters are additionally proved to spell no charged term at all --
### the whole anagram class is absent from the lexicon a word is drawn from. A scramble is a string the
### backend COMPOSES, so EVERY member of the list is checked again on the composed string at generate
### time and redrawn if it fails -- the check is not applied to the first one and assumed of the rest,
### because the reshuffle button reaches all of them. A board does not filter this and must not:
### filtering on the device would mean two different lists, and the one on the device would be the
### stale one.
###
### `answer` ships for the same offline-first reason `acceptedSolutions` does: the device adjudicates
### locally and the pack is already on it. Compare with the shared normalizer, as Missing Vowels and
### Cryptogram also do -- but NOT as every type does: Phrazle adjudicates on markGuess coming back
### all-green, because its board displays no derivation of `answer` to normalize against.
###
### `theme` IS ALWAYS SHOWN, at every difficulty. It is never hidden and it is never omitted -- hiding
### it would turn a one-answer puzzle into a several-answer one, which is a different game. It is at
### most 40 characters and at most four words.
###
### NO `hints` KEY, AND NO `themedanagrams-entry` METADATA. Both were removed on 2026-08-31 and both
### are absent rather than empty. The `kind` union no longer has a member for this type.
###
### WHAT REPLACED THEM, so a client knows what it is now responsible for. The old ladder picked three
### entries by ANSWER LENGTH, ranked once the night before: first letter of the second-longest, both
### ends of the third-longest, the whole of the longest. It could not know which words the player had
### already solved, so a player who had got the longest entry still had the strongest reveal spent on
### it. The device now picks among the entries still UNSOLVED, and escalates first letter -> both ends
### -> first three letters.
###
### THE RUNGS ARE POSITIONAL AND THEY ALWAYS WERE, which is the part worth keeping in mind if you are
### drawing this board. The scramble is on screen, so its length and its letter multiset are already
### known to the player; the only thing a rung can add is WHICH LETTER GOES WHERE. That is why every
### rung is a prefix or an end, and why a client that renders them as plain sentences and nothing else
### is leaving most of their value on the floor -- the letters they name want to be shown in position.
###
### `answer` and `scrambles` are all a device needs to compute them, and both are already here.
###
### --- end themedanagrams ---
