### THE SHAPE CHANGED ON 2026-09-07 AND THERE IS NO COMPATIBILITY PATH. `definitionSpan`, `fodderSpan`
### and `device` are GONE, and the two devices they described -- "hidden" and "anagram" -- are gone
### with them. Every stored crypticclue puzzle was one of those two, so no stored cryptic puzzle is
### serviceable and the delete-and-rebuild runbook below is REQUIRED for this change rather than
### optional. A client written against the old shape does not degrade; it finds no `device` and no
### spans at all.
###
### `clue`            the surface reading, rendered VERBATIM. Letters and single spaces only, at most
###                   120 characters -- though the real ceiling is now lower and varies: `explanation`
###                   quotes the definition and every cue out of this same string, so a charade clue
###                   tops out near 97 characters. It carries NO enumeration parenthetical -- the
###                   client renders `clue`, then ` (${enumeration.join(',')})`.
### `answer`          the solution, ONE uppercase word of 4-8 letters. Adjudicate through the shared
###                   normalizeAnswer rule, as on every other type.
### `enumeration`     word lengths, derived in code from `answer`, so it cannot disagree with it.
###                   Always length 1 today; the field is an array because the WIRE SHAPE is the
###                   expensive thing to change.
### `explanation`     THE POST-SOLVE REVEAL, rendered VERBATIM and never parsed. At most 100
###                   characters. It replaces the three fields above rather than joining them, and it
###                   is the only thing on the wire that says how the clue worked.
###
###                   IT NAMES THE DEFINITION, and that is load-bearing. The span-driven reveal it
###                   replaces always named the definition; a wordplay-only string would drop that
###                   fact for every player, since none of these devices puts its letters in the
###                   clue. A charade and a deletion QUOTE IT FIRST; a double definition takes no
###                   such prefix, because both halves ARE definitions and it already names them
###                   both. Three shapes:
###                     charade           "Floor covering" = CAR (vehicle) + PET (animal)
###                     deletion          "a mark" = BRANDY (spirit) minus its last letter
###                     doubledefinition  Two definitions: "Departed" and "still remaining"
###
###                   DO NOT STRING-SEARCH THE CLUE FOR THESE SUBSTRINGS in order to highlight them.
###                   No offsets are shipped, on purpose: a substring degrades to no highlight, an
###                   offset degrades to a WRONG one, and a wrong highlight on a cryptic clue points
###                   the player at the wrong half of the puzzle. A cue is also not always a literal
###                   slice of what it explains -- "vehicle" is in the clue, CAR is not.
###
### THE DEVICE IS NOT ON THE WIRE. Three exist -- charade, deletion, doubledefinition -- and the
### client is told which only in prose, inside `explanation` and sometimes a rung. It came off with
### the spans: it existed to drive a highlight that no longer has offsets to work with, and a field
### with no reader is a field that rots. Do not branch on the device; render the strings.
###
### `hints`           ONE TO THREE RUNGS -- the only type whose ladder VARIES in length, and the
###                   reason the shared contract above says to read `hints.length`. The other two
###                   types that still carry a ladder, gofigure and missingvowels, ship exactly three
###                   every time; the three types that carry none ship no key at all. So this is the
###                   one place a count is genuinely unknown until you look.
###
###                   THERE IS ONE POOL PER DEVICE and they do not share an order, because the same
###                   rung is worth different amounts on each. A charade always ships three. A
###                   deletion ships three unless its gloss dropped AND its definition is one word,
###                   which gives two. A double definition ships three with a gloss and two without.
###
###                   RENDER THE RUNGS IN ORDER AND DO NOT MATCH ON THEIR TEXT. A client keyed to
###                   "rung 1 is the device" reads the wrong sentence on most clues.
###
###                   THE GLOSS IS THE ONLY RUNG ABOUT THE ANSWER; every other one describes the
###                   clue. It is one sentence of at most 80 characters, model-written and gated
###                   here, and it may carry ordinary punctuation that `clue` may not. It is a RUNG
###                   and not a field: there is no `gloss` key on `data` and there will not be one.
###
###                   THE LADDER ESCALATES AND THE GIVEAWAY IS LAST. A rung that hands over the
###                   answer -- naming every charade part, or naming a deletion's source word -- sits
###                   at the bottom or is not emitted at all. On the gentlest deletion shape it is
###                   unreachable entirely. Do not reorder the rungs and do not surface a later one
###                   first; doing either hands the player the answer with their first hint.
###
###                   NO RUNG STATES THE ANSWER LENGTH, because `enumeration` is already on the wire
###                   and the client already renders it beside the clue. If you stop rendering the
###                   enumeration, the length stops being available -- say so here rather than
###                   putting it back in a rung.
###
### THERE IS NO indicatorSpan, AND NO SPAN OF ANY KIND. The API computes an indicator span internally
### -- it falls out of the same cover the verifier proves -- and deliberately does not ship it, for
### the same reason the other two spans left: a span the client never highlights is a span nothing
### would catch going wrong.
###
### `category` is ABSENT BY DESIGN rather than hidden: the definition half IS the category, it is
### always on the wire because it is inside the clue, and hiding it is not available. Do not branch
### on its absence.
###
### `clue` IS STORED BYTE-IDENTICAL to the string the API verified, and that still matters with the
### spans gone. `explanation` and the quoting rungs are composed from slices of it taken at
### verification time, so a clue needing a trim is rejected rather than trimmed. Do not trim it, do
### not collapse its whitespace, do not re-encode it.
###
### BEST EFFORT. This type is excluded from `complete`, so a pack that shipped no cryptic clue is
### still complete and the client must NOT treat a missing crypticclue puzzle as a reason to refetch.
### Render what arrives.
###
